### the two TAG patterns, which are process names and not binary strings
  fps_hal      in the UT rootfs: 0   in the vendor image: /etc/init/android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint@2.0-service.rc
 /etc/selinux/precompiled_sepolicy
 /etc/selinux/vendor_file_contexts
 /etc/selinux/vendor_sepolicy.cil
  gx_fpd       in the UT rootfs: 0   in the vendor image: /etc/init/android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint@2.0-service.rc
 /etc/selinux/precompiled_sepolicy
 /etc/selinux/vendor_file_contexts
 /etc/selinux/vendor_sepolicy.cil
 /lib64/libfpservice5118m.so
  -> they are the daemon's and the HAL's names as the vendor's init/.rc and selinux files spell
     them. A logcat line prints the process name as its tag, so these can appear in logcat without
     being in any binary -- the reason they are NOT removed with the five below.

## side B -- journal: strings in a UT-side binary, so biometryd's own journal

### setActiveGroup failed
  /mnt/utrootfs/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libbiometry.so.2.0.0
  -- Android-side hits: system 0, vendor 0

### Failed to instantiate device
  /mnt/utrootfs/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libbiometry.so.2.0.0
  -- Android-side hits: system 0, vendor 0

### Cannot construct Forwarding device
  /mnt/utrootfs/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libbiometry.so.2.0.0
  -- Android-side hits: system 0, vendor 0

### Clearing template store
  /mnt/utrootfs/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libbiometry.so.2.0.0
  -- Android-side hits: system 0, vendor 0

### Failed to enroll template
  /mnt/utrootfs/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libbiometry.so.2.0.0
  -- Android-side hits: system 0, vendor 0

### and the line the whole probe hinges on, in full
  setActiveGroup failed: %s
  -> %s /mnt/utrootfs/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libbiometry.so.2.0.0
     that is biometryd's own client library, on the UT side. The probe's header says the device
     log shows the CALLER's 'setActiveGroup failed: SYS_EINVAL' and nothing from the HAL, because
     the HAL's access(W_OK) branch logs nothing (BiometricsFingerprint.cpp:221-223). So this line
     is the evidence -- and it is in the journal, never in logcat. Counting it in logcat (which
     is what this probe did) made it structurally 0, and the inference the probe prints could
     therefore never be read off the output.

## side C -- the patterns that were REMOVED, and why a 0 for them was never evidence

### Unable to get FP service
  UT rootfs     : 0
  Android system: 0
  Android vendor: 0
  searched as a fixed string, recursively, over every regular file in the image (grep reads the
  whole file, so a name surviving only as an exported symbol would still show up here)

### Connected to IBiometricsFingerprint
  UT rootfs     : 0
  Android system: 0
  Android vendor: 0
  searched as a fixed string, recursively, over every regular file in the image (grep reads the
  whole file, so a name surviving only as an exported symbol would still show up here)
  -> and this one is worth its own line: it was ALSO the string the self-test's own logcat
     fixture contained ('Connected to IBiometricsFingerprint::2.1 service'), so the harness
     made it look real. A fixture may only contain strings that exist in an image.

### Unable to get IBiometricsFingerprint
  UT rootfs     : 0
  Android system: 0
  Android vendor: 0
  searched as a fixed string, recursively, over every regular file in the image (grep reads the
  whole file, so a name surviving only as an exported symbol would still show up here)

### Can not open fingerprint HW Module
  UT rootfs     : 0
  Android system: 0
  Android vendor: 0
  searched as a fixed string, recursively, over every regular file in the image (grep reads the
  whole file, so a name surviving only as an exported symbol would still show up here)
  the real strings, in /mnt/vendor-ro/bin/hw/android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint@2.0-service.leeco_zl1:
    Can't open fingerprint HW Module, error: %d
    Can't create instance of BiometricsFingerprint, nullptr
  -> 'Can't', not 'Can not'. Both patterns asked for a string that does not exist anywhere,
     so both counts were 0 for every possible device state -- in a probe whose subject is a
     line that is MISSING. A 0 there is not evidence; it is the pattern.

### Can not create instance of BiometricsFingerprint
  UT rootfs     : 0
  Android system: 0
  Android vendor: 0
  searched as a fixed string, recursively, over every regular file in the image (grep reads the
  whole file, so a name surviving only as an exported symbol would still show up here)
  the real strings, in /mnt/vendor-ro/bin/hw/android.hardware.biometrics.fingerprint@2.0-service.leeco_zl1:
    Can't open fingerprint HW Module, error: %d
    Can't create instance of BiometricsFingerprint, nullptr
  -> 'Can't', not 'Can not'. Both patterns asked for a string that does not exist anywhere,
     so both counts were 0 for every possible device state -- in a probe whose subject is a
     line that is MISSING. A 0 there is not evidence; it is the pattern.

## what the probe prints now, from the same three groups

  logcat  block: Bad path length / Start biometrics / Opening fingerprint hal library /
                 Can't open fingerprint HW Module / Can't create instance of BiometricsFingerprint /
                 Fp::connect failed / getService failed / fps_hal / gx_fpd
  journal block: setActiveGroup failed / Failed to instantiate device /
                 Cannot construct Forwarding device / Clearing template store /
                 Failed to enroll template

  and the pair the diagnosis rests on, now that both halves are in the log each one is in:
    'Bad path length' > 0                    -> the path never reached access() (too long / empty)
    'Bad path length' = 0 AND
    'setActiveGroup failed' > 0              -> the access(W_OK) branch, which logs nothing

## how to reproduce

  sh scripts/host/zl1-loc-fp-selftest.sh        # 179 checks, no device
  and, for the counts above, the same greps this file was produced by:
  grep -rlaF --exclude-dir=doc -- 'Bad path length' /mnt/android-sys-test /mnt/vendor-ro
  grep -rlaF --exclude-dir=doc -- 'setActiveGroup failed' /mnt/utrootfs
